To determine the total area of the shaded region in the given figure, we need to analyze the structure of the rectangle and the shaded areas.

The figure consists of a rectangle with 12 unit squares. The total area of the rectangle is:
\[
12 \text{ unit squares} \times 1 \text{ unit} = 12 \text{ square units}
\]

The figure is divided into 12 equal parts, and the shaded regions are the areas of the 12 individual squares. Therefore, the total area of the shaded region is simply the sum of the areas of the 12 unit squares.

Since each unit square has an area of 1 square unit, the total area of the shaded region is:
\[
12 \text{ unit squares} \times 1 \text{ square unit per square} = 12 \text{ square units}
\]

Thus, the total area of the shaded region is:
\[
\boxed{12}
\]

